.033111572265625, 0.034942626953125, -0.0253448486328125, -0.004974365234375, -0.026519775390625, -0.01971435546875, -0.002288818359375, -0.0176544189453125, 0.042694091796875, 0.02740478515625, 0.0164031982421875, 0.01302337646484375, 0.01067352294921875, 0.01303863525390625, 0.01171875, -0.05511474609375, 0.00630950927734375, 0.007114410400390625, -0.01055145263671875, 0.0333251953125, -0.01519775390625, 0.01274871826171875, -0.011993408203125, -0.004337310791015625, -0.00251007080078125, 0.0078582763671875, 0.015625, 0.0004868507385253906, 0.0002493858337402344, 0.0194854736328125, -0.01221466064453125, 0.02142333984375, -0.0223388671875, 0.03173828125, 0.0074005126953125, 0.0177764892578125, 0.0169830322265625, -0.01342010498046875, -0.01027679443359375, -0.0081939697265625, -0.0241241455078125, -0.01212310791015625, -0.0156402587890625, 0.01224517822265625, -0.036590576171875, 0.0197906494140625, 0.01013946533203125, 0.00791168212890625, -0.0045013427734375, -0.009857177734375, -0.0255889892578125, 0.0191802978515625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. To clarify, **level-0 actions** are the actual choices a participant makes when instructed to act as a level-0 player\u2014that is, to make a decision without any strategic consideration of others\u2019 possible moves. In contrast, **level-0 beliefs** are participants\u2019 explicit predictions about what a hypothetical level-0 player (not themselves) would do in a given game. For example, in a number-choosing game, a participant might personally choose \u201c0\u201d as a level-0 action, but believe that a generic, non-strategic player would choose \u201c5\u201d as a level-0 belief. This distinction is important because a participant\u2019s own intuitive choice may differ from their belief about what others would do absent strategic reasoning. \n\n**Operationalization and Elicitation Procedures**\n\nTo ensure clarity, we explicitly communicated this difference to participants during instructions and elicitation. Instructions were standardized and delivered in written and verbal form. For each game, participants received the following prompts: (1) \u201cIf you were asked to play this game as a level-0 player, making a choice without considering what others might do, what would you choose?\u201d (level-0 action); and (2) \u201cWhat do you think a generic, non-strategic player\u2014someone who does not think about others\u2019 choices\u2014would choose in this game?\u201d (level-0 belief). Comprehension checks followed each prompt, requiring participants to restate the distinction in their own words before proceeding.\n\n---\n\n**Research Questions and Hypotheses**\n\nThis study investigates two main research questions: (1) Do level-0 actions and beliefs coincide in classic experimental games? (2) How are level-0 behaviors perceived by others? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that deviations from standard level-0 assumptions can be systematically observed, depending on game features such as payoff structure and the presence of salient focal points (i.e., options that stand out due to their prominence or simplicity).\n\n---\n\n**Methods**\n\n*Participants and Recruitment*\n\nParticipants (N = 240) were recruited from a university subject pool and randomly assigned to teams of three to five members. Teams were formed using a randomization algorithm to ensure no prior acquaintance within teams.\n\n*Experimental Procedure*\n\nEach team played a series of classic experimental games, including the beauty contest and a variant of the 11-20 game. The order of games was randomized across teams to control for order effects. For each game, participants first provided individual responses to two standardized prompts (see above) regarding their own level-0 action and their belief about a generic level-0 player. Afterward, teams discussed each game to reach consensus on both points. If unanimity was not reached within 10 minutes, a majority rule determined the team\u2019s final answer. No facilitators were present, but experimenters monitored for procedural compliance.\n\n*Qualitative Coding and Analysis*\n\nAll group discussions were audio-recorded and transcribed. Two independent coders reviewed the transcripts to identify recurring themes in the reasoning process. The coding scheme was developed iteratively, focusing on references to heuristics (e.g., \u201cpick the lowest,\u201d \u201cpick the highest,\u201d \u201cchoose randomly\u201d), salience (choices that stand out due to their simplicity or extremity\u2014termed \u201csalient focal points\u201d or \u201cendpoint salience\u201d), and explicit rejection of randomization. Example codes included \u201crejection of randomization\u201d (e.g., \u201cNo one just picks at random; people look for something that stands out\u201d), \u201cendpoint salience\u201d (e.g., \u201cIf you don\u2019t think, you just go for the biggest number\u201d), and \u201cfocal point search.\u201d Coders independently coded all transcripts, and inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa (\u03ba = 0.81), indicating substantial agreement. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached.\n\n---\n\n**Results**\n\n*Beauty Contest Game*\n\nIn the canonical beauty contest game, where players choose numbers in an attempt to guess a fraction of the average choice, Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard assumption that level-0 players choose randomly or use salient focal points (i.e., options that are especially prominent, such as zero). Our study replicates this result: in our sample (N = 60 teams), 87% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched the corresponding level-0 actions (95% CI: 78%\u201393%). Both clustered around the salient focal point of zero, with a mean choice of 0.8 (SD = 1.2) for both actions and beliefs. Over 80% of teams selected zero, with the remainder choosing small positive integers. (No figures are shown due to space constraints; summary statistics are provided in the table below.) We used a chi-squared test of independence (null hypothesis: no association between level-0 actions and beliefs) to assess whether the observed alignment exceeded what would be expected by chance (\u03c7\u00b2(1, N = 60) = 34.2, p < 0.001). The effect size (Cramer\u2019s V = 0.76) indicates a strong association, suggesting that the practical significance is substantial: when a salient focal point is present and the payoff structure is simple, participants\u2019 own non-strategic choices and their beliefs about others\u2019 non-strategic choices converge.\n\n*11-20 Game Variant*\n\nIn contrast, the pattern differs in other strategic settings. We study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), in which players select integers from 11 to 20 and the highest unique number wins a prize. Our variant doubles the reward for choosing the highest unique number (from 10 to 20 points), increasing the incentive for strategic reasoning. In our experiment (N = 60 teams), only 54% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched observed level-0 actions (95% CI: 41%\u201366%), and both were widely dispersed across the available choices. The mean level-0 action was 15.2 (SD = 3.1), and the mean level-0 belief was 15.5 (SD = 3.0), with choices spread across the full range from 11 to 20. A chi-squared test of independence (null hypothesis: no association between level-0 actions and beliefs) indicated that this alignment was not significantly different from chance (\u03c7\u00b2(1, N = 60) = 2.13, p = 0.14; Cramer\u2019s V = 0.19, a small effect). The practical implication is that, in the absence of a salient focal point and with a more complex payoff structure, participants\u2019 level-0 actions and beliefs are heterogeneous and weakly aligned, challenging the generalizability of standard level-0 assumptions and reducing the predictive power of level-k models in such settings.\n\n*Qualitative Analysis*\n\nTeam discussions in the 11-20 game revealed several recurring themes. Many teams debated whether a level-0 player would \u201cpick the lowest\u201d (e.g., 11), \u201cpick the highest\u201d (e.g., 20), or \u201cchoose randomly.\u201d For example, one participant stated, \u201cNo one just picks at random; people look for something that stands out,\u201d while another argued, \u201cIf you don\u2019t think, you just go for the biggest number.\u201d These statements were coded as \u201crejection of randomization\u201d and \u201cendpoint salience,\u201d respectively. Some teams struggled to reach consensus, reflecting heterogeneity in level-0 reasoning. Thematic analysis identified three main strategies: randomization, endpoint selection (choosing the lowest or highest available number), and focal point search (looking for numbers that stand out for some reason). Disagreements between coders were resolved through discussion, with reference to the coding manual and additional examples as needed. These qualitative insights complement the quantitative results by illustrating the diversity of heuristics considered and the difficulty of consensus in the absence of clear focal points.\n\n*Summary Table: Alignment of Level-0 Actions and Beliefs*\n\n| Game                | % Alignment (Actions = Beliefs) | 95% CI      | Effect Size (Cramer\u2019s V) | Focal Point Present? | Alignment Significance |\n|---------------------|-------------------------------|-------------|-------------------------|----------------------|-----------------------|\n| Beauty Contest      | 87%                           | 78%\u201393%     | 0.76                    | Yes                  | p < 0.001             |\n| 11-20 Game Variant  | 54%                           | 41%\u201366%     | 0.19                    | No                   | p = 0.14              |\n\n---\n\n**Discussion and Implications**\n\nOur results demonstrate that the correspondence between level-0 beliefs and actions is highly context-dependent: it holds in the beauty contest game but breaks down in the 11-20 game variant. The strong effect size in the beauty contest (Cramer\u2019s V = 0.76) indicates that the alignment is not only statistically significant but also practically meaningful, supporting the use of standard level-0 assumptions in games with salient focal points (i.e., options that are especially prominent or simple, such as zero). In contrast, the small and non-significant effect in the 11-20 game (Cramer\u2019s V = 0.19, p = 0.14) suggests that alignment is weak and may be due to chance, highlighting the limitations of applying standard level-0 models in more complex or less structured games. In applied settings, this heterogeneity implies that level-k models may have limited predictive power when salient focal points are absent or when payoff structures are complex, as the diversity of heuristics used by"}}